@startuml fs-tenant-isolation-current-and-fix
' ============================================================================
'  WHO CAN READ WHOSE FILES - filesystem tenant isolation across the modes,
'  the unfenced-read premise that leaks one tenant's workspace and transcripts
'  to another on the hosted web, and the read-scope fix.
'  STATUS: DESIGN (nothing built yet) - companion to
'  tenant-isolation-current-and-fix.puml, which covered the EGRESS leaks
'  (event fan-out + GET /file, fixed via may_observe). This one is about the
'  TOOL layer: what read/ls/find/exec can reach inside the daemon itself.
'
'  THE BREAK, one sentence:
'    the write side has ONE fence at ONE choke point (write_scope.check_write),
'    but reads were declared exempt ("READS ARE NEVER SCOPED - reading damages
'    nothing") - a premise imported from desktop, where the machine is yours -
'    so on a hosted daemon, where EVERY tenant shares one process and one EFS,
'    any signed-in user can read the entire accounts/ tree of everyone else.
'
'  WHY THE FIX IS NOT "if web then restrict the read tool": that plants a
'  mode branch in a tool, the first of many, and every future tool has to
'  remember to plant its own. Instead the fix is the SAME shape the write
'  side already proved: one check at the choke point all paths already pass
'  through, fed by per-run DATA. The mode never appears in code - a desktop
'  run carries an EMPTY scope (= unrestricted, today's bytes), a hosted run
'  carries the caller's roots. Deployment changes values, never code paths.
'
'  Render (from this folder):
'    java -jar ../../../plantuml.jar -tpng fs-tenant-isolation-current-and-fix.puml
' ============================================================================

skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam ArrowColor #555555
skinparam ArrowFontColor #444444
skinparam ArrowFontSize 11
skinparam ActivityBorderColor #6A6A6A
skinparam ActivityBackgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam ActivityDiamondBackgroundColor #FFF4E5
skinparam ActivityDiamondBorderColor #C9A227
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFF8DC
skinparam NoteBorderColor #C9B458
skinparam partitionBorderColor #9AA0B5
skinparam partitionFontStyle bold
skinparam partitionFontSize 13

title Filesystem tenant isolation - today's unfenced reads, the observed leak, and the read-scope fix\none EFS, one daemon, many tenants: isolation must be ENFORCED at the path choke point,\nnot assumed from each tool defaulting its cwd to the right folder

start

partition "0 . TODAY'S GEOMETRY   (what is already right)" #E8F0FE {
  :ONE path authority (infrastructure/user_state.py) maps
  (account, agent) to a namespace inside the one store:
  accounts/<acct>/agents/<id>/ = definition + workspace/ + sessions/;
  :tenant identity is established ONCE per connection (the accounts
  contextvar + per-connection registry overlay) and consumed as a
  value downstream - workspace, transcripts and memory all key on
  the CALLER's account and never mix;
  :the WRITE side is fenced at one choke point:
  application/write_scope.check_write, carried on the RunContext,
  realpath containment (is_inside), deny beats allow;
  :the READ side is exempt BY DESIGN: "READS ARE NEVER SCOPED -
  reading damages nothing" (write_scope.py docstring), and the fs
  tools honor it - _resolve() maps a relative path into the run's
  workspace and passes any ABSOLUTE path straight through
  (plugins/core_fs/fs_tools.py);
}

switch (which deployment is this daemon?)

case ( desktop LOCAL / BYOK )

  partition "1 . DESKTOP - the premise is TRUE" #E6F4EA {
    :one human, one machine, files already theirs. An agent reading
    any absolute path is a FEATURE (read your repo, your Desktop,
    the skill it vendors) - nothing to isolate, nothing leaks;
  }

case ( hosted WEB - many tenants, one EFS )

  partition "2 . HOSTED - the observed leak, step by step" #FDECEA {
    :user X chats with marketing-agent: files land in
    accounts/acct_X/agents/marketing-agent/workspace/,
    transcripts in .../sessions/ - the UNIFIED layout keeps an
    agent's definition AND its user data in the same folder;
    :user Y signs in. Sidebar is clean - sessions key on the
    caller's account, so Y sees no chats of X (this part works);
    :Y runs the same agent. The run's OWN workspace resolves
    correctly to accounts/acct_Y/... (container._acct_workspace) -
    the resolver is NOT the bug;
    :but the agent's prompt names its DEFINITION directory, which
    physically lives in X's subtree - and X's workspace/ and
    sessions/ sit right inside it;
    if (Y's agent calls ls / read with that absolute path?) then (yes)
      :_resolve() passes absolute paths through untouched, no read
      check exists anywhere => X's files and chat transcripts are
      returned to Y. This is the reported incident;
    else (no - it explores instead)
      :same outcome, slower: nothing stops a walk from Y's folder
      up to accounts/ and into ANY tenant's subtree;
    endif
    :=> the incident is the MILD case. The general fact: every
    hosted user can read the whole /data tree - all tenants'
    workspaces, transcripts, uploaded files - through first-party
    tools, no exploit needed, just a path;
  }

endswitch

partition "3 . ROOT CAUSE - two facts multiply" #FDECEA {
  :FACT A: reads are unfenced ON PURPOSE, a desktop premise that
  became false the day one daemon started serving many tenants;
  :FACT B: user data lives INSIDE the shareable definition folder,
  so wherever a definition legitimately crosses a tenant boundary
  (marketplace, curated catalog, app serving, read_paths grants),
  the owner's data is standing next to it;
  :neither fact is a tool bug. Fixing a tool - or hiding one
  folder - leaves the class open. The fix must be structural:
  decide visibility ONCE, enforce it where all paths already flow;
}

partition "4 . THE FIX - the write side's shape, applied to reads" #E6F4EA {
  :MOVE 1 - THE VISIBILITY SET, decided once per run. One builder
  computes read_roots + write_roots from (caller account, resolved
  agent): the caller's OWN per-agent subtree, the agent's
  DEFINITION VIEW read-only, the shared catalog read-only, scratch.
  No account signed in => EMPTY, and empty means unrestricted;
  :MOVE 2 - check_read BESIDE check_write, same file, same
  RunContext carriage, same is_inside realpath containment (so
  symlinks and dot-dot cannot walk out). The fs tools' _resolve()
  gains ONE line; read/ls/find inherit it through the funnel they
  already share. Refusals NAME the caller's legitimate roots, so
  the model redirects instead of retrying;
  :MOVE 3 - ONE definition of "the shareable part of an agent":
  a definition-view function next to USER_DATA_DIRS (= workspace,
  sessions) in domain/agent.py. Run grants, the sandbox
  CapabilityResolver's read_paths, app serving and publish packing
  ALL consume it - so in-process tools and sandboxed plugins get
  the same answer and cannot drift. Data never crosses a tenant
  boundary; definitions do;
  :MOVE 4 - RESOLUTION SCOPING, the front door: the registry
  answers a caller only from the shared layer + the caller's own
  overlay. The remaining bare-agent_id process-global maps (the
  known open gap) get keyed by (account, agent). The fence of
  moves 1-3 is the backstop for whatever still slips through;
  :INVARIANTS riding along: exec/shell on hosted stays refused or
  runs INSIDE the subprocess sandbox under the same grant (one
  unfenced shell makes the fence decorative); sessions appear in
  NO read grant ever - transcripts are reachable only through
  session RPCs, which already pass may_observe;
}

partition "5 . WHAT EACH MODE FEELS AFTER   (same code, different values)" #E0F2F1 {
  :desktop LOCAL - unchanged, byte for byte: no account, empty
  scope, every path allowed, the check passes everything;
  :desktop CLOUD (signed in) - own roots granted, own files all
  in scope: the UX is identical, the fence only exists for paths
  that were never yours;
  :hosted WEB - Y lists files and sees Y's OWN (empty) workspace.
  Y's agent tries X's absolute path and gets a refusal naming
  Y's roots. The reported incident becomes unreproducible;
  :CI pins it: a cross-tenant probe suite - two accounts, every
  path-taking tool and RPC, each asserting refusal on the other
  tenant's subtree - so the property is PROVEN continuously, not
  promised once;
}

stop

note right
  ==== WHY THERE IS NO "if web" ANYWHERE ====
  The mode never appears in code. The builder runs for
  every deployment and produces different VALUES:
    no account        => read_roots = ()   (unrestricted)
    signed-in caller  => read_roots = that caller's roots
  check_read is mode-blind; it only asks "inside a root?".
  A future tool that resolves paths through the shared
  funnel is fenced automatically on day one - no wrapper,
  no per-tool edits, no branch to forget.

  ==== WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER (later rungs) ====
  * kernel-level enforcement (per-tenant uid/gid or EFS
    access points) - defense in depth below the process
  * gVisor / Firecracker behind the existing sandbox seam
    for third-party plugin code
  * per-tenant disk quotas (already on the open-gaps list)
  None are needed for the fence to be correct; they harden
  the layers underneath it.
end note

legend left
  | Piece | desktop today | hosted today | hosted after the fix |
  | who resolves the workspace | the run (per account) - correct | the run (per account) - correct | unchanged |
  | relative paths in tools | mapped into the run's workspace | mapped into the run's workspace | unchanged |
  | absolute paths in tools | allowed - the machine is yours | allowed - THE HOLE | inside the caller's roots only |
  | other tenants' subtrees | n/a (one tenant) | fully readable | refused, refusal names own roots |
  | agent definition sharing | n/a | carries owner's workspace + sessions | definition view only - data stays home |
  | enforcement point | write side only (check_write) | write side only | reads AND writes at one choke point |
  | proof | - | - | cross-tenant probe suite in CI |
endlegend

@enduml
